const pageData = JSON.parse("{\"subject\":\"Physics\",\"title\":\"International system of units (SI) \\u0026 scientific notations\",\"eyebrow\":\"\",\"introduction\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"contentHtml\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eInternational System of Units (SI), contains the most fundamental units of necessary physics quantities: with all these SI units, other informal units or secondary units can be easily derived.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ctable\\u003e\\n\\u003cthead\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003cth\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eQuantity\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/th\\u003e\\n\\u003cth\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eName\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/th\\u003e\\n\\u003cth\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSymbol\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/th\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\n\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eLength\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eMeter\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003em\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eMass\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eKilogram\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ekg\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eTime\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eSecond\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003es\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eElectric current\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eAmpere\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eA\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eTemperature\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eKelvin\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eK\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eAmount of substance\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eMole\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003emol\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eLuminous intensity\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eCandela\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ecd\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAs shown above, these are the current seven units of physics quantities included in International System of Units. As I've mentioned that combining these units result in a unit describing a new physical quantities (i.e. unit of velocity/speed (m/s) can be derived by distance (m) divided by time (s), no worries if you do not know these, since we will start talking about it in kinematics and motion). This is always true, and if you discover a physical quantity with its unit exceeding the capability scope of this table, then you possess a large potential to be awarded with Nobel Prize. Please tell me by then, and a great CONGRATS will be sent to you (I'm just joking).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThen, we'd probably also like to learn about scientific notation and related conversion of unit (both between scales and between metrics). Things you've noticed is that units we are using in daily life seems to be deviated from this table, and that's reasonable since this table is a concise and brief generalization of the most basic quantities and associated units (it does not cover every details). The unit that we usually use in supermarkets recording the calories of a type of snack is calorie instead of joule (J, kg*m^2/s^2); while when the community worker comes to your home reading the total amount of electricity that your family have used, the unit is kilowatt per hour. This is conceivable, since in different application scenarios, the scale vary according to the situation (you will not measure the mass of your vehicle using gram but kilogram).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAnd let's first take a look at how the same unit with different scales converts into each other. The most familiar situations that we've probably been exposed to during our childhood is the conversion of unit of length (cm, dm, m, km...). So, we can figure out that unit using the same metric (think about the spacing on a ruler, they're sharing the same distance between two adjacent lines, right?) can be conveniently converted regardless of the unit itself (we only need to care about the amount of unit). For instance, I can convert micrometer (\\\\mum) into 10^-6 m, which, in this case, the amount of the same physical quantity remains unchanged; the only things that change is the scale being used (still applying the ruler analogy, you're only switching from one ruler with smaller spacing to another with larger spacing based on tens, exponential with \\\"10\\\" as base). In this way, we can apply a conventional naming of all the tens.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ctable\\u003e\\n\\u003cthead\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003cth\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003ePrefix Name\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/th\\u003e\\n\\u003cth\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSymbol\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/th\\u003e\\n\\u003cth\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eBase 10\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/th\\u003e\\n\\u003cth\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eDecimal\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/th\\u003e\\n\\u003cth\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eEnglish Word\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/th\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\n\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eyotta\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eY\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^24\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e1,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eseptillion\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ezetta\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eZ\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^21\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e1,000,000,000,000,000,000,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003esextillion\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eexa\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eE\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^18\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e1,000,000,000,000,000,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003equintillion\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003epeta\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eP\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^15\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e1,000,000,000,000,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003equadrillion\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003etera\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eT\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^12\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e1,000,000,000,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003etrillion\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003egiga\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eG\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^9\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e1,000,000,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ebillion\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003emega\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eM\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^6\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e1,000,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003emillion\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ekilo\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eK\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^3\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e1,000\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ethousand\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ehecto\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eh\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^2\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e100\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ehundred\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003edeca\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eda\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^1\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eten\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^0\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e1\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eone\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003edeci\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ed\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^-1\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e0.1\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003etenth\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ecenti\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ec\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^-2\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e0.01\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ehundredth\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003emilli\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003em\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^-3\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e0.001\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ethousandth\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003emicro\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e/mu\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^-6\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e0.000,001\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003emillionith\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003enano\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003en\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^-9\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e0.000,000,001\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ebillionith\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003epico\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ep\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^-12\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e0.000,000,000,001\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ethrillionith\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003efemto\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ef\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^-15\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e0.000,000,000,000,001\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003equadrillionith\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eatto\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ea\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^-18\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e0.000,000,000,000,000,001\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003equintillionith\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ezepto\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ez\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^-21\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e0.000,000,000,000,000,000,001\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003esextillionith\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eyocto\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003ey\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e10^-24\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e0.000,000,000,000,000,000,000,001\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003eseptillionith\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eOkay, a very intimidating form, I admit, since some of them I never used before (it took me a while to take them down from the browser to here). Yet, do you notice the conveniency of applying the scientific notation: a extremely concise expression that simplifies the normal way which we're used to write numbers. Why use 10 as the base? This is simply because that, usually, our conventional mathematic number system operates using the \\\"10\\\" decimal. This longlasting convention still works well and has deeply ingrained in our computation. Yet we know as well that computers are using binary system, thus the things written in decimal can be translated into binary for computers to read. Why does computer adopt binary system instead of decimal? If we continue our convention here, then wouldn't it be more convenient and efficient and that we no longer needs programing? The answer is simple (despite that I feel that we're branching off from our original topic and stepping into computer science, but I'd like to resolve your concern here): computers contain sophisticated circuits and the states of circuits can only have two different possibilities: 1. a current goes through that circuit; 2. no current goes through that current. Therefore, \\\"1\\\" and \\\"0\\\" well fit into this scenario, rendering binary system to be the primary system contemporary computers rely on.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThen, we'd like to also figure out how metrics of the same physical quantities are converted between one and another. Normally, the two different metrics follow a constant-ratio relationship. For example, 1 mile is roughly equal to 1.609 km, thus 5 miles is roughly equal to 8.047(roughly 5*1.609) km. Yet, sometimes, things work differently: the SI unit for temperature is Kelvin (K), yet the two we're used to are Celsius and Fahrenheit. The conversion between each two of them work slightly differently: 1K=1+(-273.15) Celsius degree; 1 Fahrenheit degree=9/5(Fahrenheit degree/Celsius degree)*Celsius degree+32 (Fahrenheit degree); 1K=(Fahrenheit degree-32)*5/9+273.15. Thus, not every conversion follows the simplest \\\"constant-ratio\\\" condition. Yet, they're generally linear relationship, which is still acceptable. Later, we may encounter more complicated ones or not, but as long as you know the relationship between one metric and another, the conversion will work out accordingly. \\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAnd some may ask that why is the relationship between Fahrenheit degree and Celsius degree so weird. I'd like to end this section with the answer to this question. It's highly relevant to measurement: the nature or definition of measurement is indeed comparing relatively using a reference. We cannot directly know the height of the classroom door or the weight of your cat by looking at them. We need to compare it to some more uniformly defined thing, which is \\\"reference\\\". So, a short answer to this question is that the references used by the two temperature unit system are different: Celsius degree system sets zero celsius degree as the temperature liquid water begins to freeze (or in reverse, solid water (ice) begins to melt into liquid water), while setting 100 celsius degree as the temperature at which liquid water begins to boil into gaseous water. Notice that these two temperatures are set under the standard atmosphere pressure, since air pressure will affect the temperature for phase change (we will explain this later on in thermodynamics); conversely, Fahrenheit degree system sets zero fahrenheit degree as the temperature of equal ice-salt mixture, and 30 and 90 for freezing water and normal body temperature which later on switched to 32 and 96 (finally 98.6). As for Kelvin, which is also named as temperature unit of thermodynamics, its zero degree signifies the absolute value at which molecules at micro scale stops to vibrate randomly. \\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eI sincerely hope that this page helps you obtain the things that you're interested in. If there's any constructive suggestions, please contact me, thanks.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\",\"children\":[],\"parentTitle\":\"Basis of Measurement \\u0026 Units\",\"parentUrl\":\"/physics/interesting-topics/classical-mechanics/basis-of-measurement-and-units/\"}");
